1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,920
Hi everyone, and thanks for pressing play.

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:06,940
This is a quick recap of The Hartford's November 13th, 2026 HartBeat:

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:12,120
Real Talk on Real Estate, with Erin Parkinson and Chris Tosie, prepared by Stuckey & Company.

4
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:15,000
So why spend time on real estate?

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,890
Erin said it's currently her team's number one industry for new business this year,

6
00:00:18,890 --> 00:00:21,380
counting habitational and lessors' risk together.

7
00:00:21,380 --> 00:00:24,560
Her team has written right at six million dollars in new business,

8
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:28,000
and more than twenty-five million dollars in total premium year to date.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,670
Those numbers are for her team's small business book, not all of The Hartford.

10
00:00:31,670 --> 00:00:34,820
This isn't a theoretical appetite — they're already winning here.

11
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:42,080
Erin focused on three areas: habitational, lessors' risk, and property management, which is workers' comp only.

12
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,930
These are not underwritten the same way, and the biggest difference is building age.

13
00:00:46,930 --> 00:00:49,050
Let's take them one at a time.

14
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:51,720
Habitational first.

15
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:58,220
Four things: 1990 or newer, up to six stories, strong occupancy, and professionally managed and well maintained.

16
00:00:58,220 --> 00:01:01,990
Erin was firm: year built is when the foundation was poured.

17
00:01:01,990 --> 00:01:05,710
A 1970s building that's been updated still doesn't qualify.

18
00:01:05,710 --> 00:01:11,630
For a condo risk, use the habitational condominium class, not the commercial condo association class.

19
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:14,780
Get the square footage right, including garages.

20
00:01:14,780 --> 00:01:20,800
Pre-qualify a good 1990-or-newer hab account with your territory manager before you spend time in ICON.

21
00:01:21,850 --> 00:01:23,950
Now lessors' risk, or LRO.

22
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:28,050
Erin said there's a lot more flexibility here than people realize.

23
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:31,150
The 1990 guideline does not apply the same way.

24
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:35,260
Underwriters have won buildings that are even a hundred years old.

25
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:39,520
What matters is the building's story: maintenance, updates, the roof,

26
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:43,720
plumbing, electrical and HVAC, who manages it, and who occupies it.

27
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:49,300
Send a current rent roll or tenant list with each tenant's name, operation and square footage.

28
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:54,080
And underwriting's tongue-in-cheek tip: if the tenant list still shows a Sears,

29
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:58,400
a Radio Shack or a Blockbuster, it's probably time to double-check it.

30
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:02,710
Bottom line: don't let building age automatically disqualify an LRO.

31
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:05,380
A few words on pricing.

32
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:11,440
On LRO, a wind and hail deductible will often apply, and Erin's team can help you compare a fixed-dollar deductible,

33
00:02:11,440 --> 00:02:15,950
like ten thousand dollars, against a percentage deductible, so it's apples to apples.

34
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:18,880
There's flexibility on both habitational and LRO.

35
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:23,210
Apply your pricing credits, then tell the underwriter what price you need to win.

36
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:26,190
If the number still looks short, refer it in anyway.

37
00:02:26,190 --> 00:02:30,240
On a strong account, underwriting may unlock other pricing through tiering.

38
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:33,780
Erin's closing point: applying pricing can double your win rate.

39
00:02:34,830 --> 00:02:37,610
Third, property management, workers' comp only.

40
00:02:37,610 --> 00:02:39,690
Don't overlook this opportunity.

41
00:02:39,690 --> 00:02:44,040
The Hartford can consider first-party managers, who manage their own properties,

42
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:47,400
and third-party managers, who manage properties for others.

43
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:50,940
They're looking for experienced operations, quality properties,

44
00:02:50,940 --> 00:02:55,290
and clear employee duties — professional property management, not handyman work.

45
00:02:56,330 --> 00:02:58,180
A few questions came up.

46
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:03,680
There's no building-count cap for hab, and single-family homes are not considered habitational.

47
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:07,230
In wind-heavy areas, taller buildings, up to six stories,

48
00:03:07,230 --> 00:03:12,730
are preferred over a long stretch of single-story buildings, because there's less roof exposed.

49
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:16,070
Underwriting will also look at concentration of risk.

50
00:03:16,070 --> 00:03:19,920
California is closed for habitational, but LRO is open there.

51
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,980
Chris believed Florida and Oklahoma may also be restricted for hab,

52
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:27,890
but that wasn't confirmed, so check with your territory manager.

53
00:03:27,890 --> 00:03:30,300
On a historic-register hab remodel,

54
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:34,780
the 1990 foundation rule still applies — take it to your territory manager.

55
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:38,120
For older buildings, LRO is the more flexible path.

56
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:42,440
Here are the capacity limits from The Hartford's slides.

57
00:03:42,440 --> 00:03:46,490
They were on screen but not discussed, so confirm them for your account.

58
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:51,310
Habitational: up to twenty million dollars per location, and thirty million per account.

59
00:03:51,310 --> 00:03:55,840
Lessors' risk: up to fifteen million per location, and thirty million per account.

60
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:00,370
Property manager workers' comp: up to five million dollars in payroll per account.

61
00:04:01,410 --> 00:04:03,490
So, what should you do this week?

62
00:04:03,490 --> 00:04:08,010
One: pre-qualify hab or LRO with your territory manager when eligibility is unclear.

63
00:04:08,010 --> 00:04:10,450
Two: on hab, confirm the foundation year,

64
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:14,970
use the habitational condo class, and send accurate square footage, including garages.

65
00:04:14,970 --> 00:04:17,460
Three: on LRO, send a current rent roll.

66
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:20,640
Four: apply your pricing credits, name your target price,

67
00:04:20,640 --> 00:04:24,100
and refer strong accounts even if the first number looks short.

68
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:27,280
Five: look for workers' comp with your property managers.

69
00:04:28,320 --> 00:04:32,040
Looking ahead, next week's HartBeat, on November 20th,

70
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:36,850
is Let's Talk Tech, and there's no session Thanksgiving week, November 27th.

71
00:04:36,850 --> 00:04:43,630
Later topics include Restaurants, Serving up Success, and The Power Pair: Professional and Management Liability.

72
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:45,970
That's the recap.

73
00:04:45,970 --> 00:04:50,000
Erin's message: know which class you're in, give underwriting the whole story,

74
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,450
and use the pricing tools you already have.

75
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:55,980
If you'd like a hand, the Stuckey & Company team is here to help.

76
00:04:55,980 --> 00:04:57,410
Thanks for watching.

77
00:04:57,410 --> 00:04:59,200
Together, we make it happen.

